projects.description:
IOM Syria’s community stabilization and community-driven development efforts will focus on fostering social cohesion, restoring essential services, and strengthening local governance in areas affected by ongoing insecurity, displacement, and fragility. IOM will:
Support community reconciliation and peacebuilding initiatives led by civil society organizations (CSOs), community-based organizations (CBOs), and volunteer networks. 
Provide small grants to address localized drivers of tension and promote inclusive interaction across diverse groups;
Strengthen the capacity of CSOs and CBOs through training in conflict sensitivity, project management, community engagement, and peacebuilding skills, enabling them to lead inclusive recovery efforts;
Invest in the rehabilitation of essential infrastructure and strengthening of local service delivery, using participatory, community-driven approaches to ensure local ownership and sustainability;
Facilitate access to legal assistance, particularly related to housing, land, and property (HLP) rights, civil documentation, and dispute resolution in return areas;
Launch a programme to enable the temporary return of qualified Syrian professionals from abroad, placing them in sectors like health, education, legal identity systems, and governance to support skills development and institutional recovery. Particular areas of initial focus will include Health, Education and Services required for immediate response and recovery for IDPs and returnees;
Integrate MHPSS services into community dialogue, conflict mediation, and livelihood and social cohesion activities to strengthen individual and community resilience;
Promote community policing initiatives to build trust between communities and local security actors, improving local safety and reducing tensions;
Support conflict transformation activities aimed at mitigating community-level tensions and fostering social stability;
Implementation of PVE-focused community engagement activities to reduce vulnerabilities to violent extremism , especially among youth, and strengthen social cohesion in fragile areas; and
Promote livelihood opportunities for displaced and vulnerable populations, linking income generation with social cohesion and community stabilization efforts.

To meet the shelter needs of affected populations, IOM, in collaboration with its local partners, and based on the results of the multisectoral and spatial needs assessments (inclusive of gender analysis), will contribute to efforts to meet the urgent shelter, nonfood items and winterization needs of people, adopting a ‘quick release’ mechanism which would allow prepositioned items to be rapidly dispatched from partners' warehouses inside Syria. IOM will: 
Scale up assistance to meet emerging needs as they arise through rapid and dignified shelter and NFI assistance to meet seasonal challenges, particularly winterization, and ensuring that households have the resources for heating and adequate clothing;
Deliver comprehensive winter kits to ensure ability to withstand the challenges of the harsh conditions;
Provide both emergency shelter items for reception centers and areas hosting newly arrived IDPs or returnees, while expanding its provision of dignified shelters and the rehabilitation of shelters;
Provide light and medium housing and shelter repair to address the urgent shelter need; and
Implement a Settlement Approach through IOM's LOCALISE toolkit to support area-based recovery, integrating shelter, basic services, and community infrastructure to address priority needs and enhance resilience.

IOM Syria will support the country’s transition by facilitating safe and regular migration through coordination with government authorities to enhance border management and governance at the different border crossing point of Syria, with a focus on humanitarian border management, as well as improved access and infrastructure. IOM will:
Provide capacity-building support to border authorities to effectively manage cross-border movements while ensuring a humane and rights-based approach;
Develop SOPs to standardize humanitarian border management practices and improve coordination; 
Provide necessary humanitarian border management equipment, rehabilitate infrastructure, and enhance human resource capacity at Border Crossing Points (BCPs);
Improve infrastructure and provide equipment at points of entry in Syria;
Revise workflows, SOPs, and protocols related to the cross-border movement of people and goods between Syria and neighbouring countries, including on public health measures;
Provide capacity building for border officials on humanitarian border management, document verification, counter trafficking and other related topics; and
Support the deployment of additional frontline female officers to ensure gender-sensitive border governance.

IOM will continue to implement area-based mobile CCCM activities in areas of newly established sites, in collaboration with the Cluster, while also expanding in locations receiving new arrivals or returns. IOM will work closely with local authorities and community members to support the transition towards the informed and safe closure of camps by ensuring displaced families have access to adequate housing and services in areas of return. IOM will: 
Coordinate multi-sectoral assistance through inter-agency -community coordination meetings, mapping services and routine assessments; 
Establish Community Resource Centers (CRCs) or support existing ones, in return areas to serve as hubs for social cohesion, access to services, and information sharing;
Improve the living conditions in the camps/IDP sites through the implementation of regular care and maintenance activities, with a focus on infrastructure improvements, such as rehabilitating communal spaces and addressing shared needs like lighting and waste management;
Facilitate participatory community planning to empower local committees, promote inclusivity, and ensure the voices of women and marginalized groups are heard in decision-making, and provide ongoing assistance to site/camp management committees through regular guidance and mentoring for committee members, alongside delivering targeted training programmes focused on community participation and representation methods; and
Strengthen local governance through capacity-building on CCCM principles and disaster risk reduction.

